# TEXT: the numeric str-edge, and the difference between parsing "0" and
# failing to parse anything. §12 B-13 makes this an EDGE and B-14 makes an
# unconsumed failure a diagnostic.
#
# REMEASURED 2026-08-08 (gaps/GAP-081.md step 1). This row used to read
# `to(i64)("abc")` = 0 with no failure and no diagnostic, so a parse failure
# was indistinguishable from the successful parse of "0" — and it could only
# read that through the boxed lowering, because in a full-native module the
# conversion did not compile at all. It now FAULTS on input holding no number.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE CAN AND CANNOT HOLD. The runner requires exit 0 and
# compares stdout, so a faulting program cannot be a passing row here. The
# refusal is therefore asserted where an exit code can be read —
# `zig build convert-proof`, which requires "abc" and "12x" to fault and
# requires "0" to answer 0, so the two are never confused again. What this row
# keeps is the half that must stay TRUE for that proof to mean anything: a
# genuine zero still parses, and still parses as a zero.
#
# `007` is the second value because a strict parse is the kind of change that
# quietly acquires a radix. It is 7, decimal, not 7 octal and not a rejection.
# expect: 0 7
main: i64 = ()
    a = "0":to(i64)
    b = "007":to(i64)
    print("{a} {b}")
    0
end
